    98	# Rules are PATH-based on purpose: paths are cheap, stable, and reviewable, and
    99	# an attacker who renames a migration out of `migrations/` to dodge L3 has to do
   100	# something visible in the diff. Content sniffing was deliberately NOT used —
   101	# it is easy to defeat with formatting and produces false negatives that read as
   102	# safety. Deliberately blunt beats subtly wrong here.
   103	#
   104	# Ordering: first match wins per file, HIGHEST tier over all files wins overall.
   105	# Anything unrecognised is L1, never L0 — an unknown file is not evidence of
   106	# safety.
   107	# Two ways a keyword can end, and the difference is the whole false-positive story.
   108	#
   109	# _kw  — any casing, then a separator, a camelCase hump, or end of name. Used for
   110	#        the DANGEROUS words (auth, credentials, billing, worker...). AuthGuard.tsx
   111	#        and authGuard.tsx both reach L3, and a component that merely sounds like
   112	#        auth being reviewed as auth costs a review nobody regrets.
   113	# _kws — the same, EXCEPT that a PascalCase name does not count as a hump. Used for
   114	#        words that collide with ordinary React component names: ModelViewer.tsx is
   115	#        not a data model, ContextMenu.tsx is not React context, RoutePlanner.tsx is
   116	#        not a router. Those read as L3/L2 under _kw, and a floor that cries danger
   117	#        at a UI component is a floor people learn to route around — which costs far
   118	#        more than the handful of real files it would have caught.
   119	#        `models/`, `model.ts` and `Schema.ts` still match: only the HUMP form is
